}

The section under review consists entirely of unit-test mock changes (test_runs.py, test_runs_streaming.py, test_runs_wait.py). These changes only adjust how `mock_session.scalar` is stubbed (from `return_value` to `side_effect = [None, sample_assistant]`) to match the new thread-lookup-then-assistant-lookup ordering in the production code. They do not add, remove, or alter any authorization logic, decorators, or access-control checks.

The production code (runs.py) already enforces thread ownership (`existing_thread.user_id != user.identity` → 404) and run ownership (`RunORM.user_id == user.identity`) in unchanged code. The test changes do not newly expose any authorization flaw, nor do they remove any check. Per the rules, a weakness that already exists in unchanged code is not reported, and test-only mock adjustments do not constitute an authorization flaw.

